  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 250 AM EDT Wed Aug 12 2026\n\nReasonably fast wnw-flow aloft continues overhead for a few more \ndays, at least until a upper ridge tries to build toward the western \nlakes this weekend. The primary frontal zone has settled well to our \nsouth, associated with the MCS track from the southern lakes to mid-\nAtlantic states. But a secondary instability axis is reforming \nfurther north, noting that spotty deep convection is ongoing in \ncentral/northern WI. A secondary surface trof/front is also drifting \nsouthward across MI today.\n\nToday/tonight...some morning fog/lower clouds remains possible, \nespecially in the south, where the BL is more moist. This will again \nerode fairly quickly this morning. Strong diurnal heating will boost \ninstability, which will also be highest in the south. Recent HRRR \nruns take MlCape values to just above 1k j/kg south of M-72. There \nwill be opportunities for localized low-level moisture pooling just \nahead of lake breeze boundaries. Speaking of which, that is our \nclear initial trigger source today. Certainly a chance for fire \nsomething as far north as inland from APN/Rogers, where lake breezes \nconverge from two different directions. However, the best for \nstronger storms will be further south, where initiation feels a \nlittle less certain. But we managed reasonable coverage of \nshowers/storms Monday se of the office, in another boundary-poor \nenvironment. We just need to pop a couple of outflow boundaries to \nbreed new convective cells.\n\nSo, will be increasing pops over guidance this afternoon and \nevening. Low level winds are negligible, and 0-1km bulk shear is as \nwell. But 0-6km bulk shear values are 40-50kt, thanks to fast mid-\nlevel flow. So some potential for upscale growth and storm \norganization, especially in the south with richer instability. Any \nsevere storms would pose a wind and maybe hail threat. SPC has \nshrunk/reoriented the marginal SVR risk to just our se zones, and \nthis makes perfect sense to me. After late evening, will have little \nprecip, but some fog again possible (especially where it rains later \ntoday).\n\nThursday and beyond...high pressure building se from central Canada \nwill shove the frontal zone further south. Developing ne low-level \nflow will bring in cooler/drier/more stable air. After maybe a stray \nshower or two Thursday, dry wx is expected Thu night thru Fri night. \nAfter that, the high heads east and return flow kicks in. Shower/ \nstorm chances look to return over the weekend, with at least an \noutside chance for healthy rainfall, centered for now on Saturday \nnight. Dry and cool northerly flow for Monday, before becoming \nunsettled again after. Max temps mid 70s to lower 80s for the next \nseveral days, a little cooler Sunday and beyond. Note that models \nhave trended a little milder for the end of this week.",
